    <!--
      OBSOLETE FLOW VERSION, ADDED 2026-09-03 after the first failed destructive deploy
      (0Afiw000000WeQXCA0) reported 5 of its 6 errors as TaskFanoutService/the three Activity
      identity fields/Tasks_Fanned_Out__c still being "referenced elsewhere ... Flow Version -
      301iw000001Gx79". Deactivating a flow version does not drop its references; only deleting
      the obsolete version itself does.

      Member form is "<FlowApiName>-<VersionNumber>", i.e. a SPECIFIC-VERSION address, not the
      bare flow name and not a flow-definition-level delete. This is the standard Metadata API
      addressing scheme for individual flow versions (retrieve/deploy/destructiveChanges all use
      it identically), and it was proven empirically against usman-dpeg on 2026-09-03 rather than
      assumed, because a wrong form here either fails the deploy or deletes the live version:
        - `sf project retrieve start`, metadata parameter "Flow:Transaction_Task_Fanout-1", returned
          exactly ONE component, Id 301iw000001Gx79AAC - byte-identical to the Id named in the
          deploy error above (301iw000001Gx79, truncated to 15 chars by the error message). This IS
          version 1, Status Obsolete (confirmed via Tooling `Flow` query: VersionNumber=1,
          Status='Obsolete').
        - The same retrieve with metadata parameter "Flow:Transaction_Task_Fanout" (the BARE,
          unversioned form) returned Id 301iw00000438l7AAA - this is version 2, MasterLabel
          "Transaction Checklist Fanout", Status Active (Tooling: VersionNumber=2,
          Status='Active'). The bare name resolves to the ACTIVE version, NOT the obsolete one and
          NOT the whole flow definition. Using the unversioned member here would have targeted and
          deleted the live fan-out - the one thing this change must not touch.
      This directly answers the "could an unversioned member catch v2" question: yes, definitively
      - the bare name IS v2's address in this org right now. Only the versioned form isolates v1.

      Paused-interview check: queried FlowInterview scoped to this flow version
      (FlowVersionView.DurableId = '301iw000001Gx79AAC') = 0 rows, and an org-wide
      `SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM FlowInterview` = 0 rows confirms the query itself is valid and there
      is nothing running anywhere that would block a version delete (this flow is
      ProcessType=AutoLaunchedFlow, consistent with having no paused Wait-element interviews).
    -->
